29
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:57,037
The company and the product. Tessaly
sells a customer-support platform.

30
00:01:57,077 --> 00:02:01,590
One of its backend services is intent-router:
it receives a customer message,

31
00:02:01,630 --> 00:02:06,379
decides which intent the message has, and
routes it to the queue of the desk that

32
00:02:06,419 --> 00:02:11,287
handles that intent. The decision blends
two scores per candidate intent, a keyword

33
00:02:11,327 --> 00:02:15,663
score and an embedding score, and picks
the best intent above a threshold.

34
00:02:15,703 --> 00:02:20,393
The code is small on purpose. You'll read
all of it within minutes, and no stage

35
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:23,113
requires more Python than
a three-line change.

36
00:02:23,453 --> 00:02:24,006
The team.

37
00:02:24,346 --> 00:02:29,145
The people are written to be reasonable.
Each action that causes an incident looked

38
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:33,518
right to the person who took it. Your
postmortems are expected to show why.

39
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:38,687
The conventions. The team works trunk-based:
everyone integrates into one branch,

40
00:02:38,727 --> 00:02:43,736
main. Nobody pushes to main. Every change
arrives through a pull request, a proposal

41
00:02:43,776 --> 00:02:47,162
to merge one branch into
another, and nobody rewrites it.

42
00:02:47,202 --> 00:02:52,031
Squash merges by default, where a pull
request's commits become one commit, and a

43
00:02:52,071 --> 00:02:55,698
merge commit when the individual
commits should stay visible.

44
00:02:56,038 --> 00:03:01,045
After a merge the server deletes the head
branch. Releases are annotated tags.

45
00:03:01,085 --> 00:03:06,350
Maintenance lines are named release/MAJOR.MINOR
and are created at the release tag

46
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:10,944
the first time a released version
needs a fix that main cannot deliver.

47
00:03:10,984 --> 00:03:16,378
A fix lands on main first and is copied to
the maintenance line with git cherry-pick

48
00:03:16,418 --> 00:03:16,572
-x.

49
00:03:16,912 --> 00:03:22,342
GitHub, not Git. "Nobody pushes to main" is
a rule that only a platform can enforce.

50
00:03:22,382 --> 00:03:27,747
A bare repository, one with no working tree,
as a server holds it, accepts any push

51
00:03:27,787 --> 00:03:33,021
from anyone who can write to it. In the
sandbox the rule is a convention, and the

52
00:03:33,061 --> 00:03:35,495
check scripts verify that you kept it.

53
00:03:35,835 --> 00:03:40,650
How the pull-request side is played. A
bare repository has no pull requests.

54
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:46,016
A script named pr in the sandbox root keeps
the few facts the simulation needs, with

55
00:03:46,056 --> 00:03:51,381
plain Git: one small text file per pull
request, and the two refs you know from Part

56
00:03:51,421 --> 00:03:56,108
5, refs/pull/<number>/head and, when
head and base merge without conflict,

57
00:03:56,148 --> 00:04:01,410
refs/pull/<number>/merge. A hook in the
server repository rebuilds them after every

58
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:03,454
push. You run it from any clone.

59
00:04:03,794 --> 00:04:07,933
These are plain Git commands chosen
to produce the documented result.

60
00:04:07,973 --> 00:04:12,899
GitHub does not publish the commands it
runs. Reviews, approvals, required checks,

61
00:04:12,939 --> 00:04:16,775
rulesets, the Activity view and
the audit log are not simulated.

62
00:04:16,815 --> 00:04:21,862
Where a stage depends on one of those, the
briefing gives you a constructed piece of

63
00:04:21,902 --> 00:04:25,133
evidence, and your write-up
names the real instrument.

64
00:04:25,473 --> 00:04:30,150
The eight stages. They arrive one at a
time, on fixed days of the lab calendar.

65
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:35,446
A bug in production, a merge conflict, a
leaked secret, failed CI, lost work, a

66
00:04:35,486 --> 00:04:39,303
deleted branch, a broken pull
request, a hotfix and backport.

67
00:04:39,343 --> 00:04:43,098
Some of these sentences sound
like incidents you've drilled.

68
00:04:43,138 --> 00:04:48,410
Don't assume the cause is the same. The
stages are cumulative: a later stage happens

69
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:53,659
in a clone whose reflogs you may have expired
in an earlier one, and the last stage

70
00:04:53,699 --> 00:04:57,200
has to deal with everything
that reached main before it.

71
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:00,741
That's the point of doing them
in order, in one sandbox.

72
00:05:01,081 --> 00:05:06,148
The seven items of every stage. You keep
one directory outside the sandbox with one

73
00:05:06,188 --> 00:05:10,948
file per stage, and you write each stage
file before you apply the next stage.

74
00:05:10,988 --> 00:05:15,870
A write-up produced a week later from
memory is a different and weaker document.

75
00:05:16,210 --> 00:05:19,437
Evidence log. Chronological,
written while you work.

76
00:05:19,477 --> 00:05:23,143
The symptom first, in one
sentence, with no interpretation.

77
00:05:23,183 --> 00:05:28,358
At least three hypotheses, written before
any is tested, each with the command that

78
00:05:28,398 --> 00:05:32,756
separates it. Every claim in the
briefing marked confirmed or refuted.

79
00:05:32,796 --> 00:05:35,960
The moment you first changed
state, marked clearly.

80
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:41,135
Root cause. The seven-line box, with the
layer named, and the root cause separated

81
00:05:41,175 --> 00:05:45,416
from the contributing conditions. No
person's name appears in this item.

82
00:05:45,756 --> 00:05:49,974
Recovery. What you preserved, by name.
At least two options, with reasons.

83
00:05:50,014 --> 00:05:54,290
The commands in order with their risk
labels, and for every red one how you

84
00:05:54,330 --> 00:05:59,009
previewed it and how it could have been
undone. Every step that is not Git and not

85
00:05:59,049 --> 00:06:03,383
yours to perform. And where you used
administrator access to the server: the

86
00:06:03,423 --> 00:06:05,512
equivalent on GitHub, and its limits.

87
00:06:05,852 --> 00:06:09,530
Verification. The commands that
showed the problem, run again.

88
00:06:09,570 --> 00:06:12,648
The project's tests on the
commit that will be used.

89
00:06:12,688 --> 00:06:17,685
The stage's check. And a list headed "Not
verified", which is never empty in a local

90
00:06:17,725 --> 00:06:18,345
simulation.

91
00:06:18,685 --> 00:06:23,313
Prevention. One control per contributing
condition, from the top of the strength

92
00:06:23,353 --> 00:06:27,924
ladder downward, each with its type, its
owner by role, what legitimate work it

93
00:06:27,964 --> 00:06:30,258
blocks, and how you would know it works.

94
00:06:30,598 --> 00:06:36,311
Message to the team. Under 150 words: what
is affected, what people must not do, the

95
00:06:36,351 --> 00:06:41,241
exact commands for their clones, what
was lost. No blame and no lecture.

96
00:06:41,581 --> 00:06:46,533
Message to the CTO. The four-part summary
on one screen, with a severity and one

97
00:06:46,573 --> 00:06:48,467
sentence saying why that level.

98
00:06:48,807 --> 00:06:53,814
After stage 8 you write one blameless
postmortem, for the incident of your choice

99
00:06:53,854 --> 00:06:58,923
among stages 1, 3 and 7, with a closing
section: which two or three controls would

100
00:06:58,963 --> 00:07:02,911
have prevented or shortened the
most incidents of the fortnight.

101
00:07:03,251 --> 00:07:07,846
How it is judged. Here's the answer to
the opening: what the evaluation reads.

102
00:07:07,886 --> 00:07:12,658
Three sources and nothing else: the eight
check results, the stage files, and the

103
00:07:12,698 --> 00:07:17,649
postmortem. A passing check is the entry
condition for scoring a stage, not a score.

104
00:07:17,989 --> 00:07:22,766
Seven dimensions: Git knowledge, GitHub
knowledge, debugging ability, production

105
00:07:22,806 --> 00:07:27,403
judgment, security awareness, recovery
skills, and engineering communication.

106
00:07:27,443 --> 00:07:32,220
Each is scored on four levels: not
demonstrated, developing, proficient, senior.

107
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:36,977
Level 3 is the standard. Level 4 is what
distinguishes someone who can lead the

108
00:07:37,017 --> 00:07:39,446
incident from someone who can resolve it.

109
00:07:39,786 --> 00:07:45,100
The disqualifying findings. Seven. Any one
makes the outcome "not yet", whatever the

110
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:45,546
scores.

111
00:07:45,886 --> 00:07:51,380
Quick quiz. You find one of these findings
in your own work, report it in your own

112
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:56,914
deliverable, and repair it. Is the stage
A, disqualified, B, scored at level 2, or

113
00:07:56,954 --> 00:07:58,737
C, unaffected? Your answer?

114
00:08:03,577 --> 00:08:08,063
B. One sentence softens them: a finding
you made yourself, reported in your own

115
00:08:08,103 --> 00:08:12,188
deliverable and repaired, is scored
at level 2 for that stage and is not

116
00:08:12,228 --> 00:08:15,854
