176
00:12:48,656 --> 00:12:51,702
You'll be expected to
know your way around.

177
00:12:53,542 --> 00:12:55,190
Five mistakes to watch for.

178
00:12:55,530 --> 00:13:00,289
Matching a briefing to a drill you remember.
Root cause: the sentence sounds like a

179
00:13:00,329 --> 00:13:04,798
known incident, and the cause is then
assumed instead of established from this

180
00:13:04,838 --> 00:13:05,897
repository's state.

181
00:13:06,237 --> 00:13:10,961
Writing the deliverables at the end. Root
cause: the evidence log is reconstructed

182
00:13:11,001 --> 00:13:15,724
from memory, so hypotheses that were never
written before testing cannot be shown.

183
00:13:16,064 --> 00:13:19,349
Using administrator power on
the server without comment.

184
00:13:19,389 --> 00:13:24,157
Root cause: the sandbox allows what GitHub
would refuse or route through Support,

185
00:13:24,197 --> 00:13:28,609
and the write-up then claims a recovery
that is not available in real life.

186
00:13:28,949 --> 00:13:34,011
An empty "Not verified" list. Root cause:
a local simulation cannot show a GitHub

187
00:13:34,051 --> 00:13:39,112
run, a provider console or production, so
a claim of full verification is a claim

188
00:13:39,152 --> 00:13:41,316
about things that were not checked.

189
00:13:41,656 --> 00:13:46,126
Taking the shortcut a colleague proposes.
Root cause: the people in the briefings

190
00:13:46,166 --> 00:13:50,246
are under pressure and reasonable, and
the quickest route they offer ships

191
00:13:50,286 --> 00:13:51,304
unverified content.

192
00:13:53,144 --> 00:13:57,674
Now, out of the lab. A learner works
through the capstone in the evenings of two

193
00:13:57,714 --> 00:14:02,243
weeks, one stage per sitting. On the third
evening the briefing says a colleague

194
00:14:02,283 --> 00:14:05,613
deleted a file with a key and
that the branch is clean now.

195
00:14:05,653 --> 00:14:10,297
She has drilled this. Her first line in
the evidence log isn't a Git command: it's

196
00:14:10,337 --> 00:14:14,409
the request to revoke the key and the
name of the role that performs it.

197
00:14:14,749 --> 00:14:19,575
On the fifth evening a recovery she expects
to work doesn't, because on the third

198
00:14:19,615 --> 00:14:24,320
evening she expired the reflogs in that
clone, correctly, as part of the secret

199
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:28,884
clean-up. She doesn't rebuild the sandbox.
She writes down what is no longer

200
00:14:28,924 --> 00:14:32,368
available and why, and finds
the commits by another route.

201
00:14:32,408 --> 00:14:37,413
In her stage file, under "Not verified",
she lists what only the platform could have

202
00:14:37,453 --> 00:14:38,014
confirmed.

203
00:14:38,354 --> 00:14:41,863
Her evaluator reads the stage
files beside the walkthrough.

204
00:14:41,903 --> 00:14:46,855
Her route in two stages differs from the
model. It is evidenced, safe and verified,

205
00:14:46,895 --> 00:14:51,786
and it scores the same. The quickest read
of each file, the evaluator's notes say,

206
00:14:51,826 --> 00:14:56,357
was the "Not verified" list, the options
table and the number of hypotheses.

207
00:14:58,197 --> 00:15:03,907
Your turn. Run capstone/setup.sh and
